First Author: Dueñas-Jurado
Title: New models for donor-recipient matching in lung transplantations
Source number: 13  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective single-center Spanish study developed a hybrid logistic regression–product unit neural network model to support donor-recipient matching in lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) the analysis included 404 lung transplants performed over 23 years; (2) the target outcome was graft survival at 6 months; (3) the final model was a hybrid LRIPU model with 48 parameters; (4) variables associated with higher survival probability included higher pre- and post-transplant FVC, lower pre-transplant PCO2, shorter donor intubation time, and shorter ischemia time; and (5) variables associated with lower survival probability included lower pre-transplant FEV1, lower donor PaO2/FiO2, older donor and recipient age, bilobular transplant, bypass use, tailoring, hospitalized/ICU pre-transplant status, and traumatic brain injury as donor cause of death. The extraction below is based only on information explicitly reported in the uploaded article and its tables.

