Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a retrospective single-center cohort of 404 transplants from one hospital, with pediatric and some combined/multi-organ recipients excluded, which limits representativeness.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Table 1 includes post-transplant variables such as “fvc_prp” and “fev1_prp,” which would not be available for donor-recipient allocation at the time of transplant decision-making.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcome was clearly defined as “Survival of the graft after six months: 1 (YES), 0 (NO).”  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Although the paper provides the final model equation and coefficient interpretation, it does not report standard validation metrics or a clear validation design, and it does not describe missing-data handling.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly addresses AI/statistical modeling for lung donor-recipient matching and short-term graft survival in lung transplantation.

